  <section>
    <h2>Constraints carried in from Phase 0/1</h2>
    <p class="section-note">Each of these cost time this week. They are constraints, not advice.</p>
    <div class="rules">
      <div class="rule-row">
        <div class="rule-key">Latency</div>
        <div class="rule-val">
          pcap or the device's own <code>util_timeDiff</code> — <strong>never a client wall-clock
          loop</strong>. <code>fork</code>/<code>exec</code> plus MIB parsing costs ~40&nbsp;ms
          against a device that answers in 2.9&nbsp;ms.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="rule-row">
        <div class="rule-key">Comparisons</div>
        <div class="rule-val">
          Same-partition before/after, <strong>or</strong> a <code>running-config.txt</code>
          snapshot per side. The two partitions do not share configuration — one has 2 IP
          interfaces and 2 VLANs where the other has none.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="rule-row">
        <div class="rule-key">Console</div>
        <div class="rule-val">
          <code>dut-console</code> drives the <strong>Linux shell only</strong>. Feeding
          <code>moxash</code> to it hangs the console — it needs a real TTY. Recovery is
          <code>Ctrl-\</code> (SIGQUIT) via expect.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="rule-row">
        <div class="rule-key">Code</div>
        <div class="rule-val">
          None expected. If a task finds a real discrepancy, a fix task is
          <strong>inserted after it</strong> — root cause first, host-gcc syntax check, build
          handed to the operator, then reviewed. Phase 0/1 found two real defects this way.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Tasks</h2>

    <div class="tasks">

      <!-- ============ TASK 1 ============ -->
      <article class="task">
        <div class="task-head">
          <span class="task-num">Task 1</span>
          <span class="task-title">Prove column 1 comes from ISS</span>
          <span class="chip chip-gate">hard gate</span>
        </div>
        <div class="task-body">
          <p class="task-why">
            <strong>snmpwalk cannot answer this.</strong> A locally-synthesised column 1 and an
            ISS-provided one both return <code>INTEGER: 1..N</code> — byte-identical. The proof has
            to come from <em>how the value was produced</em>, not what it says.
          </p>
          <p class="task-why">
            Covers <strong>both</strong> <code>portConfigTable</code> and
            <code>portConfigLinkUpDelayTable</code>. §10.2's one-line gate names only the first;
            §3.3's 24-table list names both, and both have the identical col1-RO / col2-RW shape
            that created the risk.
          </p>

          <div class="steps">
            <div class="step">
              <div class="step-n">1</div>
              <div class="step-main">
                <div class="step-title"><span class="t">Write the provenance script</span></div>
                <div class="step-detail">
                  <code>tools/mxportdb_provenance.sh</code> — carries both non-disruptive signals.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="step">
              <div class="step-n">2</div>
              <div class="step-main">
                <div class="step-title">
                  <span class="t">Signal A — watch for the synthesis binary</span>
                  <span class="chip chip-op">operator</span>
                </div>
                <div class="step-detail">
                  <code>/usr/bin/iss_snmp_makeup_index</code> runs <strong>only</strong> on the
                  local synthetic path. If column 1 is forwarded it is never invoked.
                </div>
<pre><span class="c"># on the DUT, left running while step 3 executes</span>
while :; do ps -eo comm | grep -q iss_snmp_makeup_index \
  &amp;&amp; echo CAUGHT; sleep 0.05; done</pre>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="step">
              <div class="step-n">3</div>
              <div class="step-main">
                <div class="step-title"><span class="t">Signal B — column 1 vs column 2 latency</span></div>
                <div class="step-detail">
                  Forwarding costs ~3&nbsp;ms; the local ISS-file path costs hundreds. If both
                  columns of the same table are in the same range, both are forwarded.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="step">
              <div class="step-n">4</div>
              <div class="step-main">
                <div class="step-title"><span class="t">Repeat for portConfigLinkUpDelayTable</span></div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="step">
              <div class="step-n">5</div>
              <div class="step-main">
                <div class="step-title">
                  <span class="t">Signal C — stop ISS, read the failure signature</span>
                  <span class="chip chip-cond">conditional</span>
                  <span class="chip chip-gate">high risk</span>
                </div>
                <div class="step-detail">
                  Only if A and B were ambiguous. <strong>Both</strong> candidate paths depend on
                  ISS, so "can I still read it" proves nothing — the discriminator is
                  <em>how it fails</em>: the whole column vanishing means forwarded; per-row errors
                  with the OID still enumerable means local.
                </div>
                <div class="verdict caution">
                  <strong>Not run by default.</strong> <code>ISS.exe</code> is the entire switching
                  engine, not just SNMP. It is started with <code>&amp;</code> under a
                  <code>RemainAfterExit</code> unit, so <code>systemctl stop</code> will not track
                  it — kill by name, restart via <code>systemctl restart app_moxa_iss_init</code>.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="step">
              <div class="step-n">6</div>
              <div class="step-main">
                <div class="step-title"><span class="t">Write the verdict</span></div>
                <div class="verdict halt">
                  If either table reads <strong>LOCAL</strong>, <strong>stop the plan</strong> and
                  escalate. That is the hard gate failing, and it would mean removing
                  <code>agentx_owned.list</code> did not close the synthetic-index risk the way
                  §3.3 and §10.2 predict.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <!-- ============ TASK 2 ============ -->
      <article class="task">
        <div class="task-head">
          <span class="task-num">Task 2</span>
          <span class="task-title">Value correctness across all 17 entries</span>
          <span class="chip chip-op">needs partition switch</span>
        </div>
        <div class="task-body">
          <p class="task-why">
            Both partitions drive the <em>same physical ports</em>, so a same-window walk of
            <code>…603.1.1</code> on each should agree everywhere the value reflects hardware
            truth, and differ only where configuration differs. Reuses
            <code>walkdiff.py</code> unchanged.
          </p>
          <div class="steps">
            <div class="step">
